# cfop.rbk — well-known sequences, by the names cubers use for them.
#
# A module: only definitions, for solutions to import, e.g.
#   from cfop import sune
#   import cfop            # then cfop.sune
# Under each is the permutation it makes, as the engine prints it.
#
# Background.  CFOP is an acronym for the four steps, in order, of the
# widely used 3x3 speedcubing method (also known as the Fridrich method):
#   C - Cross: solve the four edges of the bottom layer to form a cross
#       lined up with the centers next to them.
#   F - F2L (First Two Layers): solve the four corner-edge pairs together,
#       completing the first two layers.
#   O - OLL (Orientation of the Last Layer): turn every piece of the top
#       layer so the whole top face shows one color (57 standard
#       algorithms).
#   P - PLL (Permutation of the Last Layer): move the top layer's pieces
#       to their solved places without changing their orientation (21
#       standard algorithms).
#
# Provenance, as the sources cited say (checked September 2026).  Most of
# these sequences come from CFOP.  Its steps' proposers, per the Speedsolving wiki: the cross,
# Donald Taylor (first known publication, 1978, per research by James
# Straughan); F2L, René Schoof, John Conway, David Benson, and David Seal;
# OLL and PLL, independently Hans Dockhorn, Kurt Dockhorn, and Anneke Treep
# (Netherlands) and Jessica Fridrich and Mirek Goljan (Czech Republic),
# 1981–83.  Wikipedia: the first official publication was by Anneke Treep
# and Kurt Dockhorn in 1981, and Fridrich published her approach online in
# 1997.  Who coined the name "CFOP": unknown.
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/CFOP_method
#   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CFOP_method
# For each sequence below: who is credited with it and who named it, where
# a source says; "unknown" where none does.

# Commutators on the top layer.

# Sequence: unknown.  Name: Arnaud van Galen, in his YouTube series
# "Complete beginners 3x3x3 tutorial", April 2008.
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Sexy_Move
let sexy         = commutator(R, U)
                 # (ur ub fr) (urf rdf)+ (ubr ulb)-

# Sequence: unknown.  Name: Jason Baum, who says his fiancée suggested
# it; Jeremy Fleischman, who first saw the name on Baum's website, named
# the inverse (F R' F' R) the "hedgeslammer" in 2009.
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/threads/hedgeslammer.13224/
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Sledgehammer
let sledgehammer = commutator(R', F)
                 # (ur fr fu) (urf frd)- (ufl rub)+

# Orienting the top corners.

# Sequence: the case is older than the name; James Nourse's "The Simple
# Solution to Rubik's Cube" (June 1981) solves it, on the bottom face, with
# R' D' R D' R' D2 R D2.  Name: Lars Petrus, for his method (Sune is a
# Swedish man's name).
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Sune
#   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simple_Solution_to_Rubik%27s_Cube
let sune         = R<U> U R<U2>
                 # (ur ub ul) (urf lbu)- (ufl ubr)+

# Sune's inverse.  Who named it: unknown.  (In French-speaking countries
# it's "the chair", from a Francocube mnemonic; so Sune is "Anti-Chair".)
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Sune
let antisune     = sune'
                 # (ur ul ub) (urf bul)+ (ubr ufl)-

# Cycling top corners.

# Sequence: unknown ("a common commutator applicable to many puzzles").
# Name: Lars Petrus, for his method's last-layer corner step.  Followed by
# U, it is commutator(R, U'<L'>), a 3-cycle of corners.
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Niklas
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Lars_Petrus
let niklas       = R U' L' U R' U' L
                 # (uf ur ub ul) (urf lbu rub ufl)

# The PLLs (permute the last layer), each named after a letter.  Who
# named them: reportedly Mirek Goljan, early 1980s, on Fridrich's PLL page,
# which is offline and could not be checked.  Who first published each
# sequence: unknown.  All are listed on the Speedsolving wiki, under the
# names given here; the wiki writes some with a U turn first to set up the
# case, left out here.
#   https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/PLL

# A-perm (Aa): the wiki's x R' U R' D2 R U' R' D2 R2 x', without the
# whole cube turns.
let aPerm        = R'<commutator(F, R'<B2>)>
                 # (urf ulb ubr)

# U-perms (Ua, and its inverse Ub).
let uPermA       = R U' R U R U R U' R' U' R2
                 # (uf ur ul)
let uPermB       = uPermA'
                 # (uf ul ur)

# H-perm, and Z-perm (the wiki's M' U M2 U M2 U M' U2 M2, then U' to line
# the top layer up).
let hPerm        = M2<U> U2 M2<U>
                 # (uf ub) (ur ul)
let zPerm        = M' U M2 U M2 U M' U2 M2 U'
                 # (uf ul) (ur ub)

# T-perm and J-perm (Jb).
let tPerm        = sexy R' F R2 U' R' U' R U R' F'
                 # (ur ul) (urf ubr)
let jPerm        = R U R' F' R U R' U' R' F R2 U' R' U'
                 # (uf ur) (urf ubr)
